## help
rc=0
grove.backup.restore — restore a dead grove's work onto this box

usage:
  rhx grove.backup.restore --from <grove> --of <org/repo> --tree <dir> [--into <dir>]
  rhx grove.backup.restore --from <grove> --home <.claude|.rhachet>   [--into <dir>]
  rhx grove.backup.restore --from <grove> --orphan <org>/<dir>        [--into <dir>]
  rhx grove.backup.restore --from <grove> --of <org/repo> --setaside <name> [--into <dir>]
  rhx grove.backup.restore --from <grove> --all

options:
  --from <grove>     the grove whose backup to read (required)
  --of <org/repo>    with --tree: one clone (--tree <repo>) or one worktree (--tree <dir>)
                     with --setaside: one tree a clone set aside, laid at ~/git/<org>/_setaside/<repo>/
  --orphan <o>/<d>   one worktree dir git had dropped, laid back at ~/git/<org>/_worktrees/<dir>
  --home <dir>       one home dir
  --all              every unit of this seat; a tree already here is kept
  --into <dir>       lay the unit at <dir> as a clone of its own, rather than in ~/git

it never overwrites: a present clone grafts, a home dir keeps each present file.
see what a grove left first:  rhx grove.backup.get --from <grove> --list
on a grove, run it as the seat that owns the work, from this repo's checkout:
  cd ~/git/more/dev-env-setup && rhx grove.backup.restore --from <grove> --all

